The key linuxcontainers.org signs its published image checksums with.

  LXC pre-built images <lxc-devel@lists.linuxcontainers.org>
  E7FB 0CAE C817 3D66 9066  514C BAEF F88C 22F6 E216
  RSA 4096, created 2014-01-11

This file is the trust root of every preset. `hack/preset-build.sh` verifies the
detached signature the upstream publishes beside each build's SHA256SUMS against
this key, and verifies the root filesystem archive against that checksum list,
before it packages anything.

The fingerprint is pinned separately in `hack/preset-build.sh`, and the build
refuses to run unless this file carries exactly that fingerprint. Committing the
key alone would only prove the build is consistent with whatever key is committed
next to it; pinning the fingerprint in a second place is what makes replacing
this file a change a reviewer has to notice.

The fingerprint above was confirmed out of band against two independent
keyservers - keyserver.ubuntu.com and keys.openpgp.org - which agree on the key,
its identity and its creation date.

Changing either the key or the fingerprint is a decision, not a fix. Upstream
rotating its signing key will break every preset build, loudly, which is the
correct outcome: someone has to establish that the new key is theirs, the same
way this one was established, and record that they did.

